    "id": "opt-blackbody-radiation",
    "title": "Blackbody Radiation Curves",
    "field": "Optics / Photonics",
    "plotType": "Family of spectral-radiance vs. wavelength curves, one per temperature (Planck)",
    "description": "A blackbody radiation chart plots the spectral radiance emitted by an ideal thermal emitter against wavelength, with a separate curve for each temperature, following Planck's law. Each curve is a smooth hump that rises to a peak and then tails off, and hotter objects emit more at every wavelength while their peak shifts toward shorter (bluer) wavelengths. The peak position obeys Wien's law, λ_peak·T = 2.898×10⁻³ m·K.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Wavelength",
        "unit": "nm"
      },
      {
        "name": "Spectral radiance",
        "unit": "W·m⁻³ (or normalized)"
      },
      {
        "name": "Temperature",
        "unit": "K"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "The wavelength at which a curve peaks tells you the emitter's temperature directly through Wien's law, which is how astronomers infer a star's surface temperature from its color — a peak near 500 nm implies roughly 5800 K, sun-like. A higher curve that also peaks farther left means a hotter source radiating more total power (the area under the curve grows as T⁴, the Stefan–Boltzmann law). Comparing a real source's shape to these ideal curves reveals how closely it approximates a perfect thermal emitter.",
